{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>בְּגִין דִּי סָרַבְתּוּן לְפוּמַאי.</b> According to the words of the Midrash (Numbers Rabbah 19:9), which I have written above, that He singled out the statement <b>מְרִיתֶם פִּי</b> corresponding to what He told them to recite a specific passage over the rock — HaShem chose this particular detail for the death of Aharon, for it is a complete offense on Aharon's scale, since even though Moshe did not recite it, it was incumbent upon Aharon to recite the passage, and when he did not recite it, behold, he rebelled against the word of HaShem. But regarding the three things recorded in their words, he was merely consenting to Moshe's action — that the striking was done by Moshe alone, and the bringing forth of water from the rock, which Israel did not want either, is also Moshe's action except that Aharon consented, and likewise the statement <b>הֲמִן הַסֶּלַע הַזֶּה</b> is Moshe's words alone. And you will find that this particular detail of the sin of הֲמִן הַסֶּלַע, HaShem singled it out for Moshe at the time He said to him (Deuteronomy 32:49) ״עֲלֵה וְגוֹ׳ וּמוּת וְגוֹ׳ עַל אֲשֶׁר מְעַלְתֶּם״ וְגוֹ׳, in which He explained מעל, and according to the words of the Midrash this statement comes on the particular detail of the sin of their saying <b>הֲמִן הַסֶּלַע</b> which He specified, and this particular matter — Aharon has no share in it, for why should Aharon be punished if Moshe said an improper utterance? This is fine regarding the matter of the rock and the striking: they are things that come as a commission from HaShem to Aharon through Moshe, and he may be punished for a change he makes in them. Not so if Moshe speaks improper things — Aharon is not held accountable for them; rather, HaShem included him with Moshe on account of the root of the matter in which they erred together."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The Aramaic lemma reflects Numbers Rabbah's rendering of the verse; the Hebrew script is preserved as given in the source. The word שִׁגָּיוֹן is translated literally as 'offense' following its usage in the midrashic context of rebellion/emerit."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:37.101687666Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}